<section class="p-hero"><div class="p-hero-bg"><img src="/assets/img/photos/calc-papers.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/calc-papers-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/calc-papers.webp 1200w" sizes="100vw" width="1200" height="749" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.15"></div><div class="wrap p-hero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><a href="/claim-calculators/">Calculators</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><span aria-current="page">Claim Gap &amp; Fee Projector</span></nav><p class="kicker">Free calculator</p><h1>Claim Gap &amp; Fee Projector: Is Appraisal Worth It in Pennsylvania?</h1><p class="lede">Enter the carrier’s number and yours, slide the award between them and add realistic fees. The projector shows whether appraisal is likely to leave you ahead after costs.</p></div></section>
<section class="sec tool-sec"><div class="wrap"><div class="rv">
<div class="tool" data-tool="gap">
  <div class="tool-head"><span>Claim gap &amp; fee projector</span><i>Runs in your browser</i></div>
  <div class="tool-body">
    <div class="tool-in">
      <div class="row2">
        <div><label for="g-ins">Insurer’s estimate (RCV, $)</label><input id="g-ins" name="insurer" inputmode="decimal" value="21600"><span class="hint">The replacement cost total on the carrier’s estimate.</span></div>
        <div><label for="g-own">Your estimate (RCV, $)</label><input id="g-own" name="yours" inputmode="decimal" value="36400"><span class="hint">Your contractor’s or independent line-item estimate.</span></div>
      </div>
      <div><label for="g-land">Where might the award land inside the gap?</label>
        <div class="range"><input id="g-land" type="range" name="land" min="0" max="100" step="5" value="55" data-unit="%"><output>55%</output></div>
        <span class="hint">0% means the carrier’s number holds; 100% means your full number is awarded.</span></div>
      <div class="row2">
        <div><label for="g-ft">Appraiser fee arrangement</label><select id="g-ft" name="feetype"><option value="flat">Flat fee ($)</option><option value="pct">Percent of the increase (%)</option></select></div>
        <div><label for="g-fee">Fee amount</label><input id="g-fee" name="fee" inputmode="decimal" value="2800"></div>
      </div>
      <label class="check"><input type="checkbox" name="useump" checked> Add my half of an umpire’s fee</label>
      <div><label for="g-ump">Umpire’s total fee ($, split 50/50)</label><input id="g-ump" name="umpire" inputmode="decimal" value="3200"></div>
    </div>
    <div class="tool-out" aria-live="polite">
      <div class="out-big"><small>Projected net increase to you</small><b data-o="net">$0</b></div>
      <div class="stack" aria-hidden="true"><span style="background:#C9D3EA"></span><span style="background:#FFCE00"></span><span style="background:#C4512A"></span></div>
      <div class="legend"><span><i style="background:#C9D3EA"></i>Carrier’s number</span><span><i style="background:#FFCE00"></i>Your net gain</span><span><i style="background:#C4512A"></i>Appraisal costs</span></div>
      <div class="out-rows">
        <div><span>Amount in dispute</span><b data-o="gap">$0</b></div>
        <div><span>Gap as a share of the carrier’s estimate</span><b data-o="pct">—</b></div>
        <div><span>Projected award (RCV)</span><b data-o="award">$0</b></div>
        <div><span>Gross increase</span><b data-o="gain">$0</b></div>
        <div><span>Your appraisal costs</span><b data-o="cost">$0</b></div>
      </div>
      <p class="out-msg" data-o="msg"></p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div></div>
<p class="tool-note">For planning only. Results depend on the figures you enter and your policy’s terms; they aren’t a quote, an appraisal or legal advice. Nothing you type is stored or sent.</p></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<h2>Reading the projection</h2>
<ol><li><strong>Use replacement cost totals for both estimates</strong>, before depreciation and the deductible, so you compare like with like.</li>
<li><strong>Test a range, not one number.</strong> Try the slider at 30%, 50% and 75%. Panels rarely adopt either side’s opening figure in full.</li>
<li><strong>Enter the fee you were actually quoted.</strong> Flat-fee and percentage arrangements change the answer more than people expect.</li>
<li><strong>Keep the umpire box on</strong> unless you’re confident the two appraisers will agree without one.</li></ol>
<h2>A quick illustrative example</h2>
<p>Say the insurer wrote a Lancaster County roof and gutter claim at $21,600 and your contractor’s line-item estimate is $36,400. The gap is $14,800. If a panel lands at 55% of the gap, the award is about $29,740 — an increase of roughly $8,140 before costs. Subtract a $2,800 flat fee and half of a $3,200 umpire fee and the projected net is about $3,740. These are illustrative figures, not results for any real claim.</p>
<h2>What this tool doesn’t account for</h2>
<p>Depreciation timing, policy limits, sub-limits and your deductible change what you are paid, not the size of the dispute. Use the <a href="/claim-calculators/rcv-acv-holdback-calculator/">RCV vs. ACV holdback calculator</a> for that side of the math, and check the <a href="/claim-calculators/claim-deadline-calendar/">deadline calendar</a> before you decide — a one-year suit clause doesn’t wait for a panel.</p>

<h2>Questions about this calculator</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Is the projected award a prediction?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>No. It is a planning scenario based on the numbers you enter. Real awards depend on documentation, the policy and the panel.</p></div></details><details><summary>How are umpire fees usually paid?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Most appraisal clauses have each side pay its own appraiser and split the umpire’s fee equally. Read your policy’s wording to confirm.</p></div></details><details><summary>What gap is usually worth appraising?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>There is no fixed threshold. Many owners start the conversation when the gap is more than about 10% of the carrier’s estimate or several thousand dollars.</p></div></details></div>
